  "item": "impl[CachedProvider/SourceProvider]/fn[read]",
  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "The provider overrides parse with a cached snapshot. Selected-source\n reads must deliver and fingerprint that snapshot without fetching again;\n this trait-required callback rejects a refetch. Primitive provider reads\n are covered separately, and executing this guard would fail this test."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/agent.rs#impl[CachedProvider/SourceProvider]/fn[read]` is a valid coverage-exception proposal.\n\n- **Placement:** It sits on an explicit function with a body, in fenced commentary, and it explains why the function would have zero execution. That is the form the built-in text allows.\n- **Reason is substantive:** The function is a trait-required `read` that returns `SourceError::Invalid(\"must not refetch\")`. The test's only purpose for it is to fail if the code refetches instead of using the cached snapshot. Running it would fail the test, so zero execution is expected. This is more than difficulty.\n- **Agrees with the source:** The sibling `parse` in the same `impl` is overridden. It builds the snapshot by calling `parse_snapshot` on fixed bytes and never calls `read`. `CachedProvider` is used in `cached_selected_source_reads_record_exact_delivered_contents_and_errors_add_no_input`. That test asserts that selected-source reads deliver and fingerprint the snapshot.\n- **Other reads are covered elsewhere:** Other providers' `read` methods, such as `Provider` in `agent.rs` and `Memory` in `sources.rs`, are real implementations, and I did not check how those tests exercise them. This fits the claim that primitive provider reads are covered separately.\n- **Scope:** The exception excuses only this function and not the file's threshold. No other gap applies to it.",
